#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# reverse_applied

#% text
# `reverse_applied(f)(a, b, ...)` is `f` applied to the arguments in reverse order.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
reverse_applied(f)(a, b, c)
#% output
#= f(c, b, a)

#% cell
reverse_applied(append)(4, [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# `reverse_applied(f, n)(a, b, ...)` reverses the first `n` arguments, and the last `-n` for a negative `n`. A count larger than the number of arguments leaves the call unevaluated.

#% cell
reverse_applied(f, 2)(a, b, c, d)
#% output
#= f(b, a, c, d)

#% cell
reverse_applied(f, -2)(a, b, c, d)
#% output
#= f(a, b, d, c)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `apply`, `reverse`, `operator_applied`
